WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.620
Your genome holds about 518 genes for protein kinases.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.280
Most of these enzymes take a phosphate from ATP and fasten it to a protein.

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.200
Why would cells lean so heavily on one small chemical group?

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.540
What makes a phosphate a good switch?

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:25.993
ATP sits at millimolar concentration in the cell, so a kinase rarely waits for its

6
00:00:25.993 --> 00:00:31.453
donor. The kinase takes the end phosphate off ATP, leaving ADP.

7
00:00:32.053 --> 00:00:36.713
In animal signaling, the best -studied sites are side chains that end in a hydroxyl

8
00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:39.833
group: – serine, threonine or tyrosine.

9
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:45.873
Near pH 7, the phosphate carries close to two negative charges, on a spot that had

10
00:00:45.873 --> 00:00:46.873
none.

11
00:00:46.916 --> 00:00:51.516
That charge matters, because charges help set a protein's shape and its contacts

12
00:00:51.516 --> 00:00:54.836
with partners. Adding a phosphate can shift either one.

13
00:00:55.356 --> 00:00:59.936
Every copy of a protein flickers among several arrangements, some working and some

14
00:00:59.936 --> 00:01:05.316
not. A phosphate makes the arrangements that suit its charge cheaper to occupy, so

15
00:01:05.316 --> 00:01:06.596
more copies sit in them.

16
00:01:07.156 --> 00:01:11.956
In glycogen phosphorylase, that shifts the enzyme toward its active form, yet the

17
00:01:11.956 --> 00:01:14.016
same trick inhibits glycogen synthase.

18
00:01:14.766 --> 00:01:19.686
A phosphatase takes the phosphate off again, with water, and leaves the protein

19
00:01:19.686 --> 00:01:21.066
exactly as it was.

20
00:01:21.606 --> 00:01:23.966
It does not run the kinase reaction backwards.

21
00:01:24.586 --> 00:01:27.826
It runs a different reaction, and each one goes downhill.

22
00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:32.126
Think of a whiteboard, where the marker and the eraser are different tools.

23
00:01:32.786 --> 00:01:37.726
The cell likewise regulates adding and removing independently, at the price of one

24
00:01:37.726 --> 00:01:39.606
ATP per round trip.

25
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.200
Why does a cell need phosphatases at all?

26
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.380
Think of a sink, with the tap running and the drain open, where the water level

27
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.080
depends on both. In the same way, the fraction of protein carrying a phosphate

28
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.600
depends on both enzymes.

29
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.580
A signal can raise that level by activating the kinase, or by inhibiting the

30
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.540
phosphatase. Cells do both.

31
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.430
A phosphate makes a good switch for four reasons.

32
00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.690
It adds charge, and it stays until an enzyme removes it.

33
00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:13.930
Different enzymes add it and remove it, and ATP keeps the kinase supplied.

34
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.490
So keep this rule.

35
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:20.970
A kinase writes the phosphate, a phosphatase erases it, and the balance of the two

36
00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:21.710
sets the level.
